364	  const user = await db.user.findUnique({ where: { email: parsed.data.email } });
365	  // ALWAYS spend one bcrypt comparison, even when the address does not exist.
366	  //
367	  // `!user || !(await bcrypt.compare(...))` short-circuits: with no account, bcrypt
368	  // never ran. The reply text was correctly identical either way, but the reply
369	  // TIME was not — bcrypt at cost 12 is ~250ms against a single indexed SELECT, a
370	  // gap one sample per address is enough to read. That turns the login form into
371	  // an account-enumeration oracle: "does this person bank here", answered for any
372	  // address, at scale, without ever guessing a password.
373	  //
374	  // It was materially worse while the throttle above was keyed on the
375	  // caller-supplied first hop of `x-forwarded-for`, because the rate limit that
376	  // would otherwise cap sampling could itself be stepped around per request. That
